 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Viewing Direction:** The model explicitly omits viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$ from the NeRF input. This is a design choice for datasets lacking camera poses, but it means the model cannot represent view-dependent effects like specular reflections [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Fine Network:** The standard NeRF "fine" network is discarded to reduce computational cost. This may limit the quality of fine details compared to full NeRF [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rank $k$:** The parameter $k$ controls the trade-off between expressiveness and memory. A higher $k$ increases memory usage significantly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Viewing Directions:** Standard NeRF requires viewing directions. HyperNeRFGAN omits them, assuming the training data lacks view-dependent characteristics like reflections [S1, unofficial]. If view-dependent effects are present, this simplification may reduce quality.
+*   **Fine Network:** Standard NeRF uses a "fine" network for multi-scale rendering. HyperNeRFGAN rejects the fine network and uses only the coarse network to reduce training cost [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Camera Poses:** The method is designed for datasets where camera positions are unavailable or hard to estimate. It requires only spatial coordinates $\mathbf{x}$, not ray origins or directions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is computationally expensive due to the hypernetwork generating weights for every iteration, but less expensive than full NeRF optimization per image because it uses only the coarse network and reduced hidden dimensions (128 channels, 4-8 layers) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is influenced by the rank $k$ of the modulation matrices. The source specifies $k=10$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced compared to standard NeRF-GANs due to the use of a single coarse network and reduced hidden dimensions (128 channels, 4 layers) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Controlled by the rank parameter $k$ in the FMM layers. Higher $k$ increases expressiveness but also memory usage [S1, unofficial]. The source uses $k=10$ [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:**
